(:kif (comment genlRules "(genlRules RULE-SPEC RULE-GENL) means that the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-SPEC via an application of RULE-SPEC will be a subset of the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-GENL via an application of RULE-GENL.  For example, the following holds\n\n<pre>\n   (genlRules\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt\n        (implies\n          (and\n           (cCFCardiacValveRepairProcedure ?CVRP Plication-ValvularProcedure)\n           (rdf-type ?PROC HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n           (cCF-contains ?CVD ?CVRP)\n           (cCF-contains ?PROC ?CVD))\n          (isa ?PROC Plication-ValvularProcedure)))\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt \n       (implies\n         (rdf-type ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n         (isa ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)))) <\/pre>\n\n\nHence, if one was looking to return instances of HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure, all other things being equal, there would be no need to query using both of these rules.  The rule in arg2 of genlRules here would get a superset of the answers that the rule in arg1 would return.\n ")
  :monotonicity :DEFAULT
  :original-tv :TRUE-DEF
  :original-source-file "tiny_kb.pldata"
  :original-source-line 8954
  :original-record 6561
  :original-form-levels (:HL)
  :original-variable-names ()
  :converted-variable-names ()
  :converted-row-variable-names ()
  :original-cnf (() ((comment genlRules "(genlRules RULE-SPEC RULE-GENL) means that the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-SPEC via an application of RULE-SPEC will be a subset of the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-GENL via an application of RULE-GENL.  For example, the following holds\n\n<pre>\n   (genlRules\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt\n        (implies\n          (and\n           (cCFCardiacValveRepairProcedure ?CVRP Plication-ValvularProcedure)\n           (rdf-type ?PROC HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n           (cCF-contains ?CVD ?CVRP)\n           (cCF-contains ?PROC ?CVD))\n          (isa ?PROC Plication-ValvularProcedure)))\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt \n       (implies\n         (rdf-type ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n         (isa ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)))) <\/pre>\n\n\nHence, if one was looking to return instances of HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure, all other things being equal, there would be no need to query using both of these rules.  The rule in arg2 of genlRules here would get a superset of the answers that the rule in arg1 would return.\n ")))
  :original-formula (comment genlRules "(genlRules RULE-SPEC RULE-GENL) means that the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-SPEC via an application of RULE-SPEC will be a subset of the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-GENL via an application of RULE-GENL.  For example, the following holds\n\n<pre>\n   (genlRules\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt\n        (implies\n          (and\n           (cCFCardiacValveRepairProcedure ?CVRP Plication-ValvularProcedure)\n           (rdf-type ?PROC HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n           (cCF-contains ?CVD ?CVRP)\n           (cCF-contains ?PROC ?CVD))\n          (isa ?PROC Plication-ValvularProcedure)))\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt \n       (implies\n         (rdf-type ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n         (isa ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)))) <\/pre>\n\n\nHence, if one was looking to return instances of HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure, all other things being equal, there would be no need to query using both of these rules.  The rule in arg2 of genlRules here would get a superset of the answers that the rule in arg1 would return.\n ")
  :original-prolog "'TINYKB-ASSERTION'(':TRUE-DEF',[[],[['comment','genlRules',\"(genlRules RULE-SPEC RULE-GENL) means that the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-SPEC via an application of RULE-SPEC will be a subset of the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-GENL via an application of RULE-GENL.  For example, the following holds\n\n<pre>\n   (genlRules\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt\n        (implies\n          (and\n           (cCFCardiacValveRepairProcedure ?CVRP Plication-ValvularProcedure)\n           (rdf-type ?PROC HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n           (cCF-contains ?CVD ?CVRP)\n           (cCF-contains ?PROC ?CVD))\n          (isa ?PROC Plication-ValvularProcedure)))\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt \n       (implies\n         (rdf-type ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n         (isa ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)))) <\/pre>\n\n\nHence, if one was looking to return instances of HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure, all other things being equal, there would be no need to query using both of these rules.  The rule in arg2 of genlRules here would get a superset of the answers that the rule in arg1 would return.\n \"]]],'UniversalVocabularyMt',[],/*HL*/['comment','genlRules',\"(genlRules RULE-SPEC RULE-GENL) means that the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-SPEC via an application of RULE-SPEC will be a subset of the set of binding tuples that satisfy the open (universally quantified) variables in the consequent of RULE-GENL via an application of RULE-GENL.  For example, the following holds\n\n<pre>\n   (genlRules\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt\n        (implies\n          (and\n           (cCFCardiacValveRepairProcedure ?CVRP Plication-ValvularProcedure)\n           (rdf-type ?PROC HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n           (cCF-contains ?CVD ?CVRP)\n           (cCF-contains ?PROC ?CVD))\n          (isa ?PROC Plication-ValvularProcedure)))\n      (ist CCFBridgingMt \n       (implies\n         (rdf-type ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)\n         (isa ?X HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure)))) <\/pre>\n\n\nHence, if one was looking to return instances of HeartValveRepair-SurgicalProcedure, all other things being equal, there would be no need to query using both of these rules.  The rule in arg2 of genlRules here would get a superset of the answers that the rule in arg1 would return.\n \"]).")

