  :original-cnf (() ((comment greaterThanOrEqualTo "A NumericComparisonPredicate (q.v.) that is a generalization of the mathematical greater-than-or equal-to <code>(>=)<\/code> relation to ScalarIntervals (q.v.) of all sorts, including quantitative intervals (see NumericInterval and Quantity) as well as point values (see ScalarPointValue).  <code>(greaterThanOrEqualTo VALUE1 VALUE2)<\/code> means that <code>VALUE1<\/code> is greater than or equal to <code>VALUE2<\/code> with respect to some scale that they are both on.  More precisely, there is some TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType <code>SCALE<\/code> that both <code>VALUE1<\/code> and <code>VALUE2<\/code> are instances of and either (i) <code>SCALE<\/code> is a specialization of NumericInterval (e.g. RealNumber) and the minimum (see minQuantValue) of <code>VALUE1<\/code> is greater than or equal to the maximum (see maxQuantValue) of <code>VALUE2<\/code>, (ii) <code>(followingValueOnScale VALUE2 VALUE1 SCALE)<\/code> holds, or (iii) <code>(equals VALUE1 VALUE2)<\/code> holds.\n<p>\nSee also greaterThan and lessThanOrEqualTo.")))
  :original-formula (comment greaterThanOrEqualTo "A NumericComparisonPredicate (q.v.) that is a generalization of the mathematical greater-than-or equal-to <code>(>=)<\/code> relation to ScalarIntervals (q.v.) of all sorts, including quantitative intervals (see NumericInterval and Quantity) as well as point values (see ScalarPointValue).  <code>(greaterThanOrEqualTo VALUE1 VALUE2)<\/code> means that <code>VALUE1<\/code> is greater than or equal to <code>VALUE2<\/code> with respect to some scale that they are both on.  More precisely, there is some TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType <code>SCALE<\/code> that both <code>VALUE1<\/code> and <code>VALUE2<\/code> are instances of and either (i) <code>SCALE<\/code> is a specialization of NumericInterval (e.g. RealNumber) and the minimum (see minQuantValue) of <code>VALUE1<\/code> is greater than or equal to the maximum (see maxQuantValue) of <code>VALUE2<\/code>, (ii) <code>(followingValueOnScale VALUE2 VALUE1 SCALE)<\/code> holds, or (iii) <code>(equals VALUE1 VALUE2)<\/code> holds.\n<p>\nSee also greaterThan and lessThanOrEqualTo.")
