(:kif (comment implies "A binary LogicalConnective (q.v.) that corresponds to the material implication operator of propositional calculus.  The implies relation takes two CycLSentence-Assertibles as its arguments; and the syntactic result of applying the term 'implies' to two sentences is itself a sentence, called a \"conditional\" or \"implication\".  A sentence of the form (implies ANTECEDENT CONSEQUENT) is true if and only if ANTECEDENT is false or CONSEQUENT is true (or both).  Equivalently: it is not the case that ANTECEDENT is true and CONSEQUENT is false.  For example,\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (implies\n    (knows Muffet Patches-Cat)\n    (likesAsFriend Muffet Patches-Cat))\n<\/pre>\n<p>\nmeans that if Muffet knows Patches then she likes him.\n<p>\nCf. the predicate sentenceImplies, which is not a logical connective, and is primarily used in rules involving quantification over CycL sentences.")
  :monotonicity :DEFAULT
  :original-tv :TRUE-DEF
  :original-source-file "tiny_kb.pldata"
  :original-source-line 9108
  :original-record 6639
  :original-form-levels (:HL)
  :original-variable-names ()
  :converted-variable-names ()
  :converted-row-variable-names ()
  :original-cnf (() ((comment implies "A binary LogicalConnective (q.v.) that corresponds to the material implication operator of propositional calculus.  The implies relation takes two CycLSentence-Assertibles as its arguments; and the syntactic result of applying the term 'implies' to two sentences is itself a sentence, called a \"conditional\" or \"implication\".  A sentence of the form (implies ANTECEDENT CONSEQUENT) is true if and only if ANTECEDENT is false or CONSEQUENT is true (or both).  Equivalently: it is not the case that ANTECEDENT is true and CONSEQUENT is false.  For example,\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (implies\n    (knows Muffet Patches-Cat)\n    (likesAsFriend Muffet Patches-Cat))\n<\/pre>\n<p>\nmeans that if Muffet knows Patches then she likes him.\n<p>\nCf. the predicate sentenceImplies, which is not a logical connective, and is primarily used in rules involving quantification over CycL sentences.")))
  :original-formula (comment implies "A binary LogicalConnective (q.v.) that corresponds to the material implication operator of propositional calculus.  The implies relation takes two CycLSentence-Assertibles as its arguments; and the syntactic result of applying the term 'implies' to two sentences is itself a sentence, called a \"conditional\" or \"implication\".  A sentence of the form (implies ANTECEDENT CONSEQUENT) is true if and only if ANTECEDENT is false or CONSEQUENT is true (or both).  Equivalently: it is not the case that ANTECEDENT is true and CONSEQUENT is false.  For example,\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (implies\n    (knows Muffet Patches-Cat)\n    (likesAsFriend Muffet Patches-Cat))\n<\/pre>\n<p>\nmeans that if Muffet knows Patches then she likes him.\n<p>\nCf. the predicate sentenceImplies, which is not a logical connective, and is primarily used in rules involving quantification over CycL sentences.")
  :original-prolog "'TINYKB-ASSERTION'(':TRUE-DEF',[[],[['comment','implies',\"A binary LogicalConnective (q.v.) that corresponds to the material implication operator of propositional calculus.  The implies relation takes two CycLSentence-Assertibles as its arguments; and the syntactic result of applying the term 'implies' to two sentences is itself a sentence, called a \\\"conditional\\\" or \\\"implication\\\".  A sentence of the form (implies ANTECEDENT CONSEQUENT) is true if and only if ANTECEDENT is false or CONSEQUENT is true (or both).  Equivalently: it is not the case that ANTECEDENT is true and CONSEQUENT is false.  For example,\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (implies\n    (knows Muffet Patches-Cat)\n    (likesAsFriend Muffet Patches-Cat))\n<\/pre>\n<p>\nmeans that if Muffet knows Patches then she likes him.\n<p>\nCf. the predicate sentenceImplies, which is not a logical connective, and is primarily used in rules involving quantification over CycL sentences.\"]]],'UniversalVocabularyMt',[],/*HL*/['comment','implies',\"A binary LogicalConnective (q.v.) that corresponds to the material implication operator of propositional calculus.  The implies relation takes two CycLSentence-Assertibles as its arguments; and the syntactic result of applying the term 'implies' to two sentences is itself a sentence, called a \\\"conditional\\\" or \\\"implication\\\".  A sentence of the form (implies ANTECEDENT CONSEQUENT) is true if and only if ANTECEDENT is false or CONSEQUENT is true (or both).  Equivalently: it is not the case that ANTECEDENT is true and CONSEQUENT is false.  For example,\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (implies\n    (knows Muffet Patches-Cat)\n    (likesAsFriend Muffet Patches-Cat))\n<\/pre>\n<p>\nmeans that if Muffet knows Patches then she likes him.\n<p>\nCf. the predicate sentenceImplies, which is not a logical connective, and is primarily used in rules involving quantification over CycL sentences.\"]).")

