(:kif (comment transitiveViaArg "A MetaPredicate used for stating that a given predicate behaves transitively, in a specified argument-place, with respect to a given binary predicate. <code>(transitiveViaArg PRED BINPRED N)<\/code> means that the <code>N<\/code>th argument position of <code>PRED<\/code> is \"transitive\" with respect to <code>BINPRED<\/code>.  That is, if <code>(PRED ... ARG-N ...)<\/code> and <code>(BINPRED ARG-N ARG-N-PRIME)<\/code> hold, then so does <code>(PRED ... ARG-N-PRIME ...)<\/code>.  For example, (transitiveViaArg relationAllExists genlPreds 1) holds; thus from (relationAllExists anatomicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart) and (genlPreds anatomicalParts physicalParts) it follows that (relationAllExists physicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart). See also transitiveViaArgInverse.")
  :monotonicity :DEFAULT
  :original-tv :TRUE-DEF
  :original-source-file "tiny_kb.pldata"
  :original-source-line 11684
  :original-record 7953
  :original-form-levels (:HL)
  :original-variable-names ()
  :converted-variable-names ()
  :converted-row-variable-names ()
  :original-cnf (() ((comment transitiveViaArg "A MetaPredicate used for stating that a given predicate behaves transitively, in a specified argument-place, with respect to a given binary predicate. <code>(transitiveViaArg PRED BINPRED N)<\/code> means that the <code>N<\/code>th argument position of <code>PRED<\/code> is \"transitive\" with respect to <code>BINPRED<\/code>.  That is, if <code>(PRED ... ARG-N ...)<\/code> and <code>(BINPRED ARG-N ARG-N-PRIME)<\/code> hold, then so does <code>(PRED ... ARG-N-PRIME ...)<\/code>.  For example, (transitiveViaArg relationAllExists genlPreds 1) holds; thus from (relationAllExists anatomicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart) and (genlPreds anatomicalParts physicalParts) it follows that (relationAllExists physicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart). See also transitiveViaArgInverse.")))
  :original-formula (comment transitiveViaArg "A MetaPredicate used for stating that a given predicate behaves transitively, in a specified argument-place, with respect to a given binary predicate. <code>(transitiveViaArg PRED BINPRED N)<\/code> means that the <code>N<\/code>th argument position of <code>PRED<\/code> is \"transitive\" with respect to <code>BINPRED<\/code>.  That is, if <code>(PRED ... ARG-N ...)<\/code> and <code>(BINPRED ARG-N ARG-N-PRIME)<\/code> hold, then so does <code>(PRED ... ARG-N-PRIME ...)<\/code>.  For example, (transitiveViaArg relationAllExists genlPreds 1) holds; thus from (relationAllExists anatomicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart) and (genlPreds anatomicalParts physicalParts) it follows that (relationAllExists physicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart). See also transitiveViaArgInverse.")
  :original-prolog "'TINYKB-ASSERTION'(':TRUE-DEF',[[],[['comment','transitiveViaArg',\"A MetaPredicate used for stating that a given predicate behaves transitively, in a specified argument-place, with respect to a given binary predicate. <code>(transitiveViaArg PRED BINPRED N)<\/code> means that the <code>N<\/code>th argument position of <code>PRED<\/code> is \\\"transitive\\\" with respect to <code>BINPRED<\/code>.  That is, if <code>(PRED ... ARG-N ...)<\/code> and <code>(BINPRED ARG-N ARG-N-PRIME)<\/code> hold, then so does <code>(PRED ... ARG-N-PRIME ...)<\/code>.  For example, (transitiveViaArg relationAllExists genlPreds 1) holds; thus from (relationAllExists anatomicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart) and (genlPreds anatomicalParts physicalParts) it follows that (relationAllExists physicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart). See also transitiveViaArgInverse.\"]]],'UniversalVocabularyMt',[],/*HL*/['comment','transitiveViaArg',\"A MetaPredicate used for stating that a given predicate behaves transitively, in a specified argument-place, with respect to a given binary predicate. <code>(transitiveViaArg PRED BINPRED N)<\/code> means that the <code>N<\/code>th argument position of <code>PRED<\/code> is \\\"transitive\\\" with respect to <code>BINPRED<\/code>.  That is, if <code>(PRED ... ARG-N ...)<\/code> and <code>(BINPRED ARG-N ARG-N-PRIME)<\/code> hold, then so does <code>(PRED ... ARG-N-PRIME ...)<\/code>.  For example, (transitiveViaArg relationAllExists genlPreds 1) holds; thus from (relationAllExists anatomicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart) and (genlPreds anatomicalParts physicalParts) it follows that (relationAllExists physicalParts Mammal Head-AnimalBodyPart). See also transitiveViaArgInverse.\"]).")

