(:kif (comment interArgDifferent "A MetaRelation used for stating a certain kind of necessary condition for being an element of the domain of a given Relation (see relationDomain).  Thus (to put it in terms of the semantics of CycL expressions) interArgDifferent can be used for stating a certain kind of truth- or denotation-condition for a given relation-denoting expression.  (interArgDifferent RELN N M) means that RELN (if it is a Predicate or other TruthFunction) only holds among or (if it is a Function-Denotational) only has a value for sequences of arguments whose Nth and Mth members are distinct (see different).  That is (assuming without loss of generality that N < M): if RELN is a predicate or other \"truth-function\" then (RELN ... ARGN ... ARGM ...) holds only if ARGN and ARGM are distinct, and if RELN is a function then it is defined for (i.e. associates a value with) a sequence <THING-1, ..., THING-N, ..., THING-M, ...> only if THING-N and THING-M are distinct.  For example, (interArgDifferent opponentsInConflict 1 2) means that no-one is his or her own opponent in a conflict.\n<p>\nNote that this predicate trivially holds for any relation with respect those argument-places it has that are subject to mutually disjoint (see disjointWith and cf. cosatisfiableInArgs) argument-type constraints (see ArgTypePredicate).")
  :monotonicity :MONOTONIC
  :original-tv :TRUE-MON
  :original-source-file "tiny_kb.pldata"
  :original-source-line 9183
  :original-record 6680
  :original-form-levels (:HL)
  :original-variable-names ()
  :converted-variable-names ()
  :converted-row-variable-names ()
  :original-cnf (() ((comment interArgDifferent "A MetaRelation used for stating a certain kind of necessary condition for being an element of the domain of a given Relation (see relationDomain).  Thus (to put it in terms of the semantics of CycL expressions) interArgDifferent can be used for stating a certain kind of truth- or denotation-condition for a given relation-denoting expression.  (interArgDifferent RELN N M) means that RELN (if it is a Predicate or other TruthFunction) only holds among or (if it is a Function-Denotational) only has a value for sequences of arguments whose Nth and Mth members are distinct (see different).  That is (assuming without loss of generality that N < M): if RELN is a predicate or other \"truth-function\" then (RELN ... ARGN ... ARGM ...) holds only if ARGN and ARGM are distinct, and if RELN is a function then it is defined for (i.e. associates a value with) a sequence <THING-1, ..., THING-N, ..., THING-M, ...> only if THING-N and THING-M are distinct.  For example, (interArgDifferent opponentsInConflict 1 2) means that no-one is his or her own opponent in a conflict.\n<p>\nNote that this predicate trivially holds for any relation with respect those argument-places it has that are subject to mutually disjoint (see disjointWith and cf. cosatisfiableInArgs) argument-type constraints (see ArgTypePredicate).")))
  :original-formula (comment interArgDifferent "A MetaRelation used for stating a certain kind of necessary condition for being an element of the domain of a given Relation (see relationDomain).  Thus (to put it in terms of the semantics of CycL expressions) interArgDifferent can be used for stating a certain kind of truth- or denotation-condition for a given relation-denoting expression.  (interArgDifferent RELN N M) means that RELN (if it is a Predicate or other TruthFunction) only holds among or (if it is a Function-Denotational) only has a value for sequences of arguments whose Nth and Mth members are distinct (see different).  That is (assuming without loss of generality that N < M): if RELN is a predicate or other \"truth-function\" then (RELN ... ARGN ... ARGM ...) holds only if ARGN and ARGM are distinct, and if RELN is a function then it is defined for (i.e. associates a value with) a sequence <THING-1, ..., THING-N, ..., THING-M, ...> only if THING-N and THING-M are distinct.  For example, (interArgDifferent opponentsInConflict 1 2) means that no-one is his or her own opponent in a conflict.\n<p>\nNote that this predicate trivially holds for any relation with respect those argument-places it has that are subject to mutually disjoint (see disjointWith and cf. cosatisfiableInArgs) argument-type constraints (see ArgTypePredicate).")
  :original-prolog "'TINYKB-ASSERTION'(':TRUE-MON',[[],[['comment','interArgDifferent',\"A MetaRelation used for stating a certain kind of necessary condition for being an element of the domain of a given Relation (see relationDomain).  Thus (to put it in terms of the semantics of CycL expressions) interArgDifferent can be used for stating a certain kind of truth- or denotation-condition for a given relation-denoting expression.  (interArgDifferent RELN N M) means that RELN (if it is a Predicate or other TruthFunction) only holds among or (if it is a Function-Denotational) only has a value for sequences of arguments whose Nth and Mth members are distinct (see different).  That is (assuming without loss of generality that N < M): if RELN is a predicate or other \\\"truth-function\\\" then (RELN ... ARGN ... ARGM ...) holds only if ARGN and ARGM are distinct, and if RELN is a function then it is defined for (i.e. associates a value with) a sequence <THING-1, ..., THING-N, ..., THING-M, ...> only if THING-N and THING-M are distinct.  For example, (interArgDifferent opponentsInConflict 1 2) means that no-one is his or her own opponent in a conflict.\n<p>\nNote that this predicate trivially holds for any relation with respect those argument-places it has that are subject to mutually disjoint (see disjointWith and cf. cosatisfiableInArgs) argument-type constraints (see ArgTypePredicate).\"]]],'UniversalVocabularyMt',[],/*HL*/['comment','interArgDifferent',\"A MetaRelation used for stating a certain kind of necessary condition for being an element of the domain of a given Relation (see relationDomain).  Thus (to put it in terms of the semantics of CycL expressions) interArgDifferent can be used for stating a certain kind of truth- or denotation-condition for a given relation-denoting expression.  (interArgDifferent RELN N M) means that RELN (if it is a Predicate or other TruthFunction) only holds among or (if it is a Function-Denotational) only has a value for sequences of arguments whose Nth and Mth members are distinct (see different).  That is (assuming without loss of generality that N < M): if RELN is a predicate or other \\\"truth-function\\\" then (RELN ... ARGN ... ARGM ...) holds only if ARGN and ARGM are distinct, and if RELN is a function then it is defined for (i.e. associates a value with) a sequence <THING-1, ..., THING-N, ..., THING-M, ...> only if THING-N and THING-M are distinct.  For example, (interArgDifferent opponentsInConflict 1 2) means that no-one is his or her own opponent in a conflict.\n<p>\nNote that this predicate trivially holds for any relation with respect those argument-places it has that are subject to mutually disjoint (see disjointWith and cf. cosatisfiableInArgs) argument-type constraints (see ArgTypePredicate).\"]).")

